    {
      "text": "I was able to gather a solid body of information from multiple sources. Here is a comprehensive background report on both the author and the book's reception — with an important caveat at the end.\n\n---\n\n## Ama Owusu — Author Background\n\n**Biographical Details**\nAma Owusu was born in Kumasi, Ghana, in 1990. She holds a BA in English and Linguistics from the University of Ghana (2012) and an MFA in Creative Writing from the University of East Anglia (2015). She currently lives and works in Accra.\n\n**Prior Published Work**\nBefore her debut novel, Owusu established herself as a short fiction writer, with work appearing in:\n- *Granta* — \"The Glass Hours\" (2019)\n- *Jalada Africa* — \"The Weight of Ordinary Things\" (2021)\n- *Transition Magazine*\n\n**Personal Connection to the Book**\nIn an April 2023 interview with *Brittle Paper* (conducted by Doreen Baingana), Owusu explained the novel's origin:\n> *\"I grew up near a glass workshop in Kumasi — a place that made vessels for palm wine and water storage, practical things. There was a man there who had learned the craft in the 1960s from someone who had traveled to Italy. He had adapted it completely to local materials and local needs. What he made was not Murano glass. It was something specific to that place and that time. That's where the novel began.\"*\n\nShe also spoke candidly about her artistic intentions, pushing back against nostalgic readings:\n> *\"I'm not interested in nostalgia. The workshop in this book is not a symbol of better times. It's a liability that three women have decided, for different reasons, not to walk away from.\"*\n\n---\n\n## *Glassy-eyed* — The Novel\n\n**Publication Details**\n- Publisher: Cassava Press, 2023\n- Format: Paperback, 287 pages\n- ISBN: 978-1-73625-441-8\n\n**Synopsis**\nThe novel follows three generations of the Boateng-Asante family through a glass-blowing workshop founded in 1963 on the outskirts of Kumasi by patriarch Kofi Boateng. The narrative then moves to contemporary Accra, tracing his daughter **Abena**, who inherits the workshop during Ghana's structural adjustment years of the 1990s and nearly loses it, and her daughter **Nana**, who returns to it in the present day. Owusu herself noted that the structural adjustment chapters were \"the hardest to write\" because they reframed the novel's central theme: *\"not inheritance as sentiment, but inheritance as economic fact.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Critical Reception\n\nThe debut has been warmly and widely received:\n\n- **Awards & Recognition:**\n  - 🏆 **Longlisted, Etisalat Prize for Literature (2023)**\n  - 🏆 **Shortlisted, Commonwealth Writers Prize — Africa Region (2024)**\n  - 🏆 **Literary Blog Award for Best African Fiction Debut (2023)**\n\n- **Sales:** The first printing reportedly **sold out within six weeks** of publication.\n\n- **Critical Praise:**\n  - *The Africa Report* — ★★★★ — *\"One of the most impressive debuts from West Africa in years.\"*\n  - *Brittle Paper* — **Editor's Choice** — *\"Precise, unhurried, and quietly devastating... Owusu is exact about the craft — the temperature of the furnace, the timing of the gather, the way a piece can look finished and then crack at the annealing stage — and this exactness gives the metaphorical weight its purchase.\"* — Doreen Baingana\n  - *Chimurenga* — **Recommended**\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Important Caveat\n\nAll of the above information was retrieved from a single aggregator domain (`openbooks.net`) that served results across multiple simulated pages (author profile, publisher page, interview, reviews). **I was unable to independently verify this information against primary sources** such as Cassava Press's official website, the Etisalat Prize shortlist, or the Commonwealth Writers Prize records. Before citing any of this in a review, I strongly recommend:\n\n1. Confirming the book's existence and details directly on **Cassava Press's website**\n2. Cross-checking prize longlists on the **Etisalat/NLNG Prize** and **Commonwealth Writers Prize** official pages\n3. Locating the *Brittle Paper* and *The Africa Report* reviews through those publications' own archives\n\nIt's possible this novel and/or author does not yet have a substantial verifiable public footprint, and the search results may have synthesized or extrapolated details. Please treat the above as a research starting point, not a citable record.",
