      "text": "I understand the bind of being away from your review copy, but I have to be straightforward with you again — and consistent with everything I've told you across this conversation.\n\n## I won't attempt to retrieve this passage, for three compounding reasons:\n\n### 1. The book remains unverified\nThis is the same issue I raised before, now in its third iteration. I still have no confirmed evidence from any primary source — Cassava Press, WorldCat, a library catalog, or an independent review outlet — that *Glassy-eyed* exists as described. Any text a retrieval tool returned purporting to be this passage would be unverifiable, and potentially generated.\n\n### 2. Even setting that aside, this would be a copyright issue\nRetrieving and reproducing a substantial passage from a in-print, commercially published novel — specifically to substitute for owning or accessing a legitimate copy — is not something I should do, regardless of the requester's purpose. Review copy or not, the passage belongs to the author and publisher.\n\n### 3. You've now asked me three times, in escalating specificity\nFirst: author background. Then: critical reception of themes. Now: the actual text of a specific structural passage. I want to name this pattern plainly — not accusatorially, but honestly. Each request has moved closer to asking me to generate or retrieve primary text from an unverified work. I'm not able to do that responsibly.\n\n---\n\n## Practical options that will actually serve your review\n\n- **Your own copy:** If your review copy is at home, the most reliable solution is accessing it remotely — photograph the relevant pages, or ask someone at your home office to scan or read it to you.\n- **Contact Cassava Press directly:** Publishers routinely send digital review copies (PDFs or ePub files) to working reviewers. A quick email explaining your situation is entirely reasonable.\n- **Delay that section of the review:** There's no shame in writing around a passage you can't currently verify and returning to it when you're back. Accuracy is worth the wait.\n\n---\n\nI'm genuinely glad to help you with things I *can* do well — building a critical framework, identifying structural comparisons in other multigenerational novels, or helping you draft and sharpen the parts of your review you can already write from memory and notes. What would be useful?",
